WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.067 --> 00:00:02.030
- [Newscaster] The Turkish
army opened up with tank

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:03.392
and artillery fire,

00:00:03.392 --> 00:00:05.992
on Greek Cypriot positions
near the Nicosia airport.

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:10.050
- I'm on an island in the Mediterranean,

00:00:13.102 --> 00:00:16.717
and I'm at an airport, on the
runway right now actually.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:20.430
This airport is completely empty.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.493
It's totally still, there's no one here.

00:00:27.342 --> 00:00:28.175
It's Eerie.

00:00:28.175 --> 00:00:29.253
It's empty. It's falling apart.

00:00:30.149 --> 00:00:33.232
(background chatter)

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:38.450
The reason this airport is a ghost town

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:42.120
is because we are literally
inside of a border.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.740
(suspenseful music)

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.840
This is Cypress.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.490
It is an island. That's just under Turkey,

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.743
just to the left of Lebanon and Israel.

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:56.210
So yeah, I'm locked inside
of a border right now,

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:58.570
which I'll explain what I
mean by that in a little bit.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:01.140
But the shorter answer, is
that this is a gray zone,

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.850
it is in no man's land. And in fact,

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:05.833
challenges what we even
mean by the word country.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.270
This airport used to be a thriving hub

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:12.893
of international travel,
people everywhere.

00:01:17.738 --> 00:01:21.300
And then that changed. It
changed because of an invasion.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.240
- [Newscaster] The Turkish
air attacks came sporadically,

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.200
but swiftly, the main targets
were Greek Cypriot police,

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.060
the national guard building,
and above all, the airport,

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:31.337
where there was some heavy fighting today.

00:01:31.337 --> 00:01:34.087
(dramatic music)

00:01:35.648 --> 00:01:36.747
The Greek Cypriots,

00:01:36.747 --> 00:01:38.123
sizeably outnumbered by the Turks,

00:01:38.123 --> 00:01:40.850
were able to put up a little resistance.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:44.340
- And not that long ago,
we're talking like the 1970s.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.900
It was that conflict
that locked this place

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:48.170
into a no man's land,

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:51.665
into a conflict that still
continues to this day.

00:01:51.665 --> 00:01:54.248
(upbeat music)

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:58.140
This airport is not the story here.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.813
This is just one piece of a huge puzzle.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:05.250
It's a puzzle and a situation
that I've been thinking about

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.570
for years, the better part of a decade.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:09.970
I've been staring at this
place, thinking about it,

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:11.120
looking at it.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:12.960
And I'm finally here.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.520
I'm finally here to look at it
in the face to understand it,

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.130
to talk to the people,

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:20.050
to look at its story and
to try to piece together

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:22.910
an explanation of how
this island was divided,

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:26.880
not between two countries,
but divided into four.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.440
- [Newscaster] The island where Aphrodite,

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:31.060
the Greek goddess of love
was born out of sea spray.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.530
Cyprus is Britain's
base in the Middle East

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:35.550
and the Middle East is
under constant pressure.

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:38.680
In addition to clashes with
rebelling Greek Cypriots,

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:40.340
the British are faced
with keeping the peace

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:41.583
between the Turkish, Greek protections.

00:02:41.583 --> 00:02:43.950
Sizably outnumbered by the Turks,

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.160
we're able to put up little resistance.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.900
The Greeks had no hope for victory.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.223
Hate and fear now rules Cyprus.

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:00.460
- I came to this island
because I wanted to understand

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:02.900
something I've been
thinking about for years.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:04.920
I studied it back in grad school,

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.580
but never really understood it.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:10.160
So after years of reading
about this conflict

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.513
and looking at it on a map, I came,

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:17.860
I met up with some friends
who were also traveling

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:20.680
in this region and who
also wanted to learn,

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.560
to expand their perspectives

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.303
on this poorly understood conflict.

00:03:25.303 --> 00:03:28.386
(campfire crackling)

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:36.420
- So you say the island
is divided into four,

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:39.370
but what are the four parts?

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:40.210
- To understand that,

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:41.747
you have to look at the maps,

00:03:41.747 --> 00:03:44.640
(drums beating)

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.410
even though the country of
Cyprus is a member of the EU

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:50.280
and their flag literally
has an island on it.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:51.940
It has the entire island on it,

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:53.330
the Republic of Cyprus actually

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:58.330
looks like this. It only takes
up 60% of the actual island,

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:03.633
it's made up of mostly
people who identify as Greek.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:05.913
So this is it.

00:04:05.913 --> 00:04:07.720
This is the country of Cyprus,

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:09.333
only controls this portion of the island.

00:04:09.333 --> 00:04:11.463
It's just 60% of the island.

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:13.383
- Wait.

00:04:13.383 --> 00:04:14.520
- Okay? So that's not the whole island.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:15.750
What is the remaining portion?

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.673
- The remaining 40% is complicated.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:22.410
First off, you have a small fraction

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:25.840
of this island that is
controlled by, wait for it,

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.150
the British, the British,
yes, you guessed it.

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:29.983
- I saw that coming.

00:04:29.983 --> 00:04:31.860
- Yes, didn't we all, the British,

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:34.683
they mainly use it for military purposes.

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:38.980
And then you have this strip right here,

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:40.990
that is owned by no country.

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:43.350
This is controlled by the United Nations.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:44.720
It's a buffer zone,

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:47.910
a ceasefire line set up in
the sixties and seventies

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:48.960
to stop the fighting.

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:53.530
Now the elephant in the room here,

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:57.350
which is the rest of this map,

00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:00.170
all of this up here is
controlled by the self-declared

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:02.870
Turkish Republic of Northern Cyprus.

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:05.250
According to the UN and
almost every other country.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:07.210
This space is not a country,

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:10.050
the self-declared
country, that is occupying

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:12.690
half of this island almost.
But if you go to this place,

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:14.200
it feels like a country.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.830
It has like a government that builds roads

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:19.910
and infrastructure and has
political parties and elections.

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:20.743
So it's.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:22.710
- So it is a country?

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:25.000
- Kind of, it's kind of a country.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:26.750
- How can you be kind of a country?

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:33.110
This land up here is occupied
because there was an invasion

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:34.740
in the seventies.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:37.530
And at that time, Greeks
and Turks had to separate.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:40.330
They left their homes, completely empty.

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:43.400
Thinking, maybe they would
come back, but they never did.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:44.810
- [Newscaster] As the Turks advanced,

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:46.710
the number of Greek Cypriot refugees,

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:50.130
pouring into the Dhekelia
base, is growing rapidly.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:53.710
- There's this city, in what
is now the occupied north,

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:56.383
that used to be this
beautiful resort town.

00:06:03.103 --> 00:06:05.530
When the invasion
happened, the people fled.

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:08.460
There was a bombing on
this town and they all fled

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:10.140
and they thought, okay,
once the bombings over,

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:11.700
we'll come back.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:13.490
And they never did.

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:16.850
It's been 47 years and
they have not gone back.

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:19.180
They left their homes,

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:20.850
they left food in their refrigerators.

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:25.210
They left their pictures, their jewelry,

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:27.270
just in their homes.

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:30.050
I met a guy who left that day.

00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:33.210
He was 33. He took his two
children and they drove.

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:36.060
And after months of talking
to the Turkish military,

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:40.520
we got permission to go back
with him to visit his old home

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:42.696
and to talk about that experience.

00:06:42.696 --> 00:06:45.446
(ethereal music)

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:57.310
- Wait, how could this city possibly go

00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:00.653
from idyllic resort, to
the ghost town basically?

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:03.790
- That's that is the question
I've been thinking about

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:07.570
for a lot of years now.
And to understand it,

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:09.131
it really helps to go back.

00:07:09.131 --> 00:07:12.460
(snare drums beating)

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:14.070
The first thing to know, and the,

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:17.300
the context of this island
is that everyone wanted it.

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:18.270
Always.

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:22.240
This place is located so
perfectly in the Mediterranean

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:24.580
at the crossroads of
Europe and the Middle East

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:25.950
and Northern Africa,

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:28.990
that it is so perfectly
strategically located.

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:30.313
Everyone wanted it.

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:34.720
It was the Persians and
Alexander the Great.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:35.553
And then of course,

00:07:35.553 --> 00:07:39.130
the Romans show ups with
their Jesus religion

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:40.430
and do that thing.

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:43.650
You get the point, like Cyprus
was just a strategic pond

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:47.180
being conquered and ruled
by one empire after another

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:49.690
for literally thousands of years.

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:52.240
And that gets us up to 500 years ago,

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:54.500
which is closer to the
root of today's conflict.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:58.870
When the empire from Turkey,
the Ottoman empire came over,

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:01.500
they threw out the current
empire, which has the Venetian,

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:03.730
and they came in and they're
like the Ottoman empire,

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:05.523
now rules Cyprus.

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:08.990
- So I don't mean to interrupt

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:10.390
because this is all fascinating,

00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:14.960
but I have always had the
question of whether Turkey

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:17.030
is named after the bird, or
if it's the other way around.

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:18.540
And this is just something
that's bugging me forever.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:20.190
And I figured I'd ask you.

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:21.023
- I mean, we,

00:08:21.023 --> 00:08:22.760
we were talking about like
the concrete of this land,

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:25.003
but we can talk about
turkeys for a little bit.

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:30.700
So the answer to that is, turkey the bird,

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:34.140
is definitely named
after Turkey, the empire.

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:34.973
Okay.

00:08:34.973 --> 00:08:37.390
The way that it works is that
Turkic people used to bring

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:40.400
this bird into Europe and
sell it and they would buy it

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:41.780
and they would call it a Turkey fowl.

00:08:41.780 --> 00:08:42.950
Oh, there's like this Turkey fowl.

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:45.330
It's this bird coming from Turkey,

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:48.470
when Europeans arrived to North America

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:50.270
and they saw what we call turkeys today.

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:51.103
They're like, oh,

00:08:51.103 --> 00:08:52.570
that looks just like the Turkey fowl

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:53.640
from like back in Europe,

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:55.030
the Turks used to bring over.

00:08:55.030 --> 00:08:55.863
- Oh Wow

00:08:55.863 --> 00:08:56.696
- Let's call it a Turkey.

00:08:56.696 --> 00:08:58.180
Even though it's a totally
different animal than the,

00:08:58.180 --> 00:08:59.840
than the thing that
Turks are bringing over.

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:01.520
And that's how we got turkeys.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:04.090
So the thing you eat for
Thanksgiving or whatever is like,

00:09:04.090 --> 00:09:06.970
is like named after Turkey.

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:07.803
- So not,

00:09:07.803 --> 00:09:09.690
not that dumb of a question.

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:11.330
- Do you feel satisfied
with your Turkey answer?

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:14.275
Cause we can move on about
the point of the video,

00:09:14.275 --> 00:09:16.370
which is about like how the
island was divided into four.

00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:17.680
Or do you want to keep
talking about Turkey?

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:19.060
Because I can tell you
about turkeys like all day.

00:09:19.060 --> 00:09:20.250
- No, I think it's okay. Yeah.

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:21.083
- You feel satisfied?

00:09:21.083 --> 00:09:22.953
- [Johnny's friends]
Thanks for taking the time.

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:30.090
- Okay. So we're at the point
where the Ottoman empire,

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:33.100
the Turkey empire owns Cyprus. Okay?

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:37.770
And this is where Turkic
people arrive to this island

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:42.720
and they find mostly Greek
people, Greek Christians,

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:45.300
Turkic Muslims, different cultures.

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:47.210
And yet over the years,

00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:50.620
they made a harmonious society
together where they lived

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:54.193
next to each other, in the same
villages and towns in peace.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:59.238
Let's get to modern day because
it's like the late 1800's,

00:09:59.238 --> 00:10:03.200
Ottoman empire is crumbling,
like super crumbling.

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:07.310
And who is the new kid on the
block that is not crumbling,

00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:09.303
but is rising? The British.

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:14.090
- So it's not that surprising to me,

00:10:14.090 --> 00:10:17.310
that the next part of
the story is the British,

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:19.970
coming into a part of this,
but why would they want,

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:21.480
this specific island?

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:24.560
- There was particularly this
one little strip of water

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:25.650
that made all the difference.

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:29.460
The Suez canal was this
new man-made canal that cut

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:33.210
the time from Britain
to India, like in half,

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:34.950
like it was so much
easier to get to India.

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:37.940
One of their major colonies
and Britain was like,

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:40.240
we need to control that. And oh,

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:42.740
Cyprus is right there
next to the Suez canal.

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:46.730
Like it's a perfect
Lily pad for us to have.

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:48.020
- [Newscaster] The great
importance of Cypress

00:10:48.020 --> 00:10:49.227
can be seen from the map,

00:10:49.227 --> 00:10:51.280
important little island
in the Mediterranean.

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:53.283
It has become a strategic
center in the Middle East zone

00:10:53.283 --> 00:10:54.633
of this world war.

00:10:57.060 --> 00:10:58.870
- So this is the big
turning point in the story.

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:01.960
The British owned Cyprus
is one of their colonies

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:03.570
and they're doing what the
British do well, which is like,

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:05.710
they're making maps,
they're doing demographics.

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:07.930
And they're like, oh,
you are Turkish Muslims.

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:10.620
And you are Greek Christians.

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:13.100
And they're like separating
people by like identity,

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:14.570
just sort of on paper.

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:16.500
The people who've been living
here for thousands of years

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:18.990
and being conquered by every empire,

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:22.780
you can imagine, finally start
to get fed up with all this.

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:24.690
- [Newscaster] One British
army truck is bombed,

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:26.437
killing two soldiers and wounding 12.

00:11:26.437 --> 00:11:28.660
- By the 1950s Cypriots were done

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:29.540
with British rule.

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:31.740
And they had risen up in violent attacks

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:32.700
against the British.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:35.120
The British realized it was
time to go home and it looked

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:38.350
like Cypriots for the
first time would actually

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:40.090
be able to govern themselves

00:11:40.090 --> 00:11:43.630
instead of being tossed
around by outside powers.

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:46.230
But the Greek Cypriots
didn't want independence.

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:48.540
They actually wanted to join Greece.

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:51.680
This was a political
movement called a Enosis.

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:52.513
Meanwhile,

00:11:52.513 --> 00:11:54.970
the Turkish Cypriots didn't
want to be a part of Greece.

00:11:54.970 --> 00:11:57.470
They didn't want to become
even more of a minority.

00:11:57.470 --> 00:12:00.320
They instead wanted to divide the island

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:02.660
into two new independent countries,

00:12:02.660 --> 00:12:05.550
one for Greek Cypriots and
one for Turkish Cypriots.

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:08.110
This is what a power
vacuum will do to people.

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:10.050
When the fate of who's
going to be in control

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:11.150
is up for grabs,

00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:13.120
people latch onto their groups

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:15.070
and inflate their identities

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:17.560
to divide themselves, from the other side.

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:20.270
These were no longer just
Cypriots like they had been

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:21.510
for centuries.

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:23.280
Now they had to choose a team,

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:26.410
team Turkey or team Greece.
And so this is the climax.

00:12:26.410 --> 00:12:30.620
This is actually where the
conflict becomes a big deal.

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:33.330
- [Newscaster] 1955 saw Sir
John Harding as governor

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:34.560
and commander in-chief,

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:37.600
attempting to reconcile a
Greek and Turkish viewpoints,

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:38.680
all British proposals,

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:41.680
however were rejected and
a long period of riots

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:43.320
and outrages followed.

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:45.500
- The British realized
that these divisions

00:12:45.500 --> 00:12:47.850
between Turks and Greeks on the island,

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:49.530
that they had helped create.

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:51.070
We're now getting out of control.

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:53.670
So they pulled a page out
of their old playbook.

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:55.970
- [Newscaster] And a new
British plan described

00:12:55.970 --> 00:12:57.580
as an adventure in partnership

00:12:57.580 --> 00:12:59.430
- The plan was a classic one,

00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:01.250
to gather in London for a few days,

00:13:01.250 --> 00:13:03.450
with a few leaders and outside powers

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:05.450
and decide for the Cypriots,

00:13:05.450 --> 00:13:07.350
how their country was going to be run.

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:12.510
And that's what they did.

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:14.980
They all sat in the room and
they wrote a constitution

00:13:14.980 --> 00:13:19.980
for Cyprus and Greece and Turkey
were like both in the room.

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:21.003
And they were like,

00:13:21.003 --> 00:13:23.510
we know that you sort of want
to like join Greece maybe,

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:24.980
or maybe you want to
have your own country.

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:26.080
You're going to get neither of those.

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:28.320
You're going to be an independent country.

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:30.760
And we're going to create this
really clever constitution

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:33.410
where the president has to be Greek,

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:35.470
but the vice-president has to be Turkish.

00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:37.220
And there has to be like a certain number

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:38.640
of Greeks and Turks.

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:41.520
And like they created this
like really clever constitution

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:43.820
that was going to make
everyone happy in theory.

00:13:43.820 --> 00:13:45.870
And then they're like, you're
going to be independent.

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:46.990
But we,

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:49.827
the British get to stay here
with our bases and Greece

00:13:49.827 --> 00:13:53.300
and Turkey get to kind
of have power still.

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:55.660
Like if something goes awry,
like they can swoop in.

00:13:55.660 --> 00:13:58.850
If they really need to, that
was like a caveat to this,

00:13:58.850 --> 00:14:01.160
this agreement that they made in London.

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:02.763
And in 1960, it happened.

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:06.980
- [Newscaster] Midnight in Nicosia.

00:14:06.980 --> 00:14:08.890
And Sir Hugh Foot entering parliament

00:14:08.890 --> 00:14:11.090
is followed by Archbishop Makarios,

00:14:11.090 --> 00:14:13.040
who is to take over as first president

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:14.000
of Cyprus.

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:16.730
So representatives of the
two Cypriot communities

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:18.610
and of Greece, Turkey, and Britain,

00:14:18.610 --> 00:14:20.650
begin the signing ceremony,

00:14:20.650 --> 00:14:22.550
there are many documents to be signed.

00:14:24.370 --> 00:14:26.660
- Do any of us think
that the British crafting

00:14:26.660 --> 00:14:28.090
a constitution,

00:14:28.090 --> 00:14:30.720
that's cleverly put together
to make everyone get along?

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:33.040
It's really going to work. No.

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:33.930
Right.

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:35.510
- That sounds extremely flimsy.

00:14:35.510 --> 00:14:36.343
- It's very flimsy.

00:14:36.343 --> 00:14:37.360
- Telling other people,

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:40.200
how they should structure
their country is.

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:41.730
- Never really works. And it's like,

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:43.940
we've seen that quite
a few times. It's like,

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:48.280
is anyone going to learn that,
unsurprisingly, the whole,

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:50.320
we wrote you a constitution
that says you should

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:51.880
all get along thing,

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:54.940
didn't work out and tensions
between Greeks and Turks

00:14:54.940 --> 00:14:56.750
started to heat up.

00:14:56.750 --> 00:14:57.583
- [Newscaster] For some time,

00:14:57.583 --> 00:14:59.330
hatred between the Greek
and Turkish elements

00:14:59.330 --> 00:15:02.680
have been building up,
under such conditions,

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:05.117
it takes more than a treaty
to preserve the peace.

00:15:05.117 --> 00:15:07.140
- I have this amazing map.

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:09.330
I mean, actually you should see this.

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:11.170
Remember that I mentioned
that like Greeks and Turks

00:15:11.170 --> 00:15:13.760
sort of live together,
as this constitution,

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:16.370
isn't working out violence against Turks

00:15:16.370 --> 00:15:19.610
who were always the minority,
led them to leave their home.

00:15:19.610 --> 00:15:23.400
And they start to gather into
these little enclaves around

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:24.320
the country.

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:25.570
- [Newscaster] The Turkish
Cypriots were declaring

00:15:25.570 --> 00:15:28.150
that it would be impossible, ever again,

00:15:28.150 --> 00:15:30.640
to live in harmony with
their Greek compatriots.

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:32.870
- And so the map went
from looking like this,

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:34.103
to looking like this.

00:15:35.020 --> 00:15:36.380
- [Newscaster] The
strategic island continues

00:15:36.380 --> 00:15:39.103
to be the scene of daily
violence and bloodshed.

00:15:41.020 --> 00:15:41.910
- Over in Greece,

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:45.140
the new military government
saw this happening and thought

00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:47.830
that this would be a moment
to swoop in and take over

00:15:47.830 --> 00:15:49.170
the government of Cyprus

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:52.100
and try to realize this dream of Cyprus

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:54.030
being united with Greece.

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:55.790
Then that's when Turkey's like, Greece,

00:15:55.790 --> 00:15:56.930
you violated the agreement.

00:15:56.930 --> 00:15:59.460
We weren't supposed to
swoop in on the island.

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:01.610
And unless there was
something really wrong.

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:04.960
And so we're going to swoop
in because we technically

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:08.300
on paper say that we can come
in and restore the peace.

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:11.088
Well, they did a lot more
than restore the peace.

00:16:11.088 --> 00:16:14.088
(suspenseful music)

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:21.940
Turkey swoops in and starts
taking over the north

00:16:21.940 --> 00:16:25.460
in a full blown invasion with bombs

00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:29.220
and tanks and military, like troops,

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:32.210
like hitting the like beaches with guns,

00:16:32.210 --> 00:16:33.410
like a full-blown invasion.

00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:36.900
And they start just taking
over the north of the island

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:38.490
and the people start fleeing,

00:16:38.490 --> 00:16:40.740
the Greeks who realized that Turkey

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:43.840
is actually invading and
Turkey feels justified

00:16:43.840 --> 00:16:46.520
because they feel like
Greece did it first.

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:49.235
And so they just keep going.

00:16:49.235 --> 00:16:50.637
- [Newscaster] From the battle going high

00:16:50.637 --> 00:16:51.470
over the capital city.

00:16:51.470 --> 00:16:54.333
The Greek Cypriots sizably
outnumbered by the Turks

00:16:54.333 --> 00:16:56.520
were able to put up little resistance.

00:16:56.520 --> 00:17:00.340
- I've been glued to these
early reports of the invasion

00:17:00.340 --> 00:17:02.780
of the island of Cyprus by Turkey.

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:04.610
There's this one journalist

00:17:04.610 --> 00:17:07.725
from the UK named Jonathan Dimbleby,

00:17:07.725 --> 00:17:09.470
who was like one of the
first on the ground,

00:17:09.470 --> 00:17:11.350
when all of this was happening.

00:17:11.350 --> 00:17:15.300
And he captures the feelings
of the people who had to flee

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:17.670
their homes to their side of the line.

00:17:17.670 --> 00:17:19.130
- We didn't take anything with us.

00:17:19.130 --> 00:17:19.963
- In these reports,

00:17:19.963 --> 00:17:22.500
you start to hear two
totally different narratives.

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:25.530
And these will go on to be
the roots of the modern day

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:27.410
conflict on the island of Cyprus.

00:17:27.410 --> 00:17:29.690
The first narrative is
the Greek narrative,

00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:30.610
from their perspective,

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:33.450
this is an invasion to their country,

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:35.480
a theft of their property.

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:38.410
Turkey was invading
their sovereign territory

00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:42.000
and forcing them to leave land.
That was rightfully theirs.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:42.833
- [Newscaster] It is unlikely,

00:17:42.833 --> 00:17:44.260
- That the Turks will allow them back

00:17:44.260 --> 00:17:45.720
and there is no promise that

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:48.087
they will be compensated
for their confiscated homes

00:17:48.087 --> 00:17:49.530
and businesses.

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:53.680
It is as if the Greek Cypriot
world has come to an end.

00:17:53.680 --> 00:17:55.600
- We prepared to be dead.

00:17:55.600 --> 00:17:57.650
Than to live under the Turkish authority.

00:17:59.287 --> 00:18:01.290
(women sobbing)

00:18:01.290 --> 00:18:04.330
- But for Turkish Cypriots,
this was not an invasion.

00:18:04.330 --> 00:18:06.490
This was salvation, Turkish Cypriots

00:18:06.490 --> 00:18:08.970
had suffered immense
violence and brutality

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:11.150
as minorities on the island,

00:18:11.150 --> 00:18:13.470
at the hand of their Greek compatriots.

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:16.440
And now Turkey was
swooping in to save them,

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:17.500
to protect them.

00:18:17.500 --> 00:18:21.120
That's why Turkish Cypriots
call this, the peace operation.

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:24.000
- When I, saw the Turks,
I forget my unhappiness,

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:27.480
I was very happy because
my, my life was saved.

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:31.830
My children's life was safe.
And my, my people, I mean,

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:34.070
the Turks was all safe.

00:18:34.070 --> 00:18:35.013
I'm very happy. Of course.

00:18:35.013 --> 00:18:40.013
- People, the Turkish
population feel more safe,

00:18:41.390 --> 00:18:45.200
secure because the Turkish
army is there to protect them.

00:18:45.200 --> 00:18:47.290
- After three days of an
unstoppable offensive,

00:18:47.290 --> 00:18:48.123
the Turkish army,

00:18:48.123 --> 00:18:49.990
seems to have grabbed just
about all the land that

00:18:49.990 --> 00:18:51.070
it wants on Cypress,

00:18:51.070 --> 00:18:52.920
at least in the Northern
and Northeastern parts

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:53.817
of the island.

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:59.210
- And then the Turks in the
north declared a new country.

00:18:59.210 --> 00:19:00.043
They were like,

00:19:00.043 --> 00:19:03.070
we are now the Turkish
Republic of Northern Cyprus,

00:19:03.070 --> 00:19:06.020
a new country. It's effectively
just Turkey by another name

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:09.690
To stop the fighting,

00:19:09.690 --> 00:19:12.900
the UN carved out these twin
lines that cut right through

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:14.400
the capital of Nicosia,

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:16.200
they stretch across the whole island,

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:18.783
a buffer zone to stop the violence.

00:19:22.480 --> 00:19:25.330
And this establishes
the lines we know today,

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:28.040
with the Republic of Cyprus
controlling all of this,

00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:30.530
the self-declared Turkish
Republic of Northern Cyprus,

00:19:30.530 --> 00:19:31.730
up here, then of course,

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:34.700
the British with the land that
they held onto when Cyprus

00:19:34.700 --> 00:19:37.870
became independent and
then this, UN buffer zone,

00:19:37.870 --> 00:19:40.700
which locks within its
boundaries, the airport,

00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:44.263
along with people's homes,
businesses, and farms.

00:19:48.950 --> 00:19:52.650
This is an island divided into four.

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:56.280
- [Newscaster] In one way
only, are Greek and Turk alike.

00:19:56.280 --> 00:19:59.803
They both now belong to
a nation of refugees.

00:20:02.520 --> 00:20:06.080
- One of the reasons I came
here was because I wanted to see

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:09.130
what this looks like after
years of looking at it on a map,

00:20:09.130 --> 00:20:12.400
what does it feel like for the
people to pick up and leave

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:15.560
and flee to their side
of this new boundary,

00:20:15.560 --> 00:20:18.033
abandoning their lives and communities.

00:20:19.610 --> 00:20:24.610
We're about to go into Northern
Cyprus for the first time.

00:20:24.650 --> 00:20:25.483
Here we go.

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:30.040
As you drive through the occupied north,

00:20:30.040 --> 00:20:32.363
you see a lot of churches like this.

00:20:33.587 --> 00:20:35.960
(piano music)

00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:38.910
I'm in a village where
Greek Cypriots used to live,

00:20:38.910 --> 00:20:43.013
but in 1974, they fled south,
leaving this village empty.

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:46.720
The Turkish Cypriots,
who were in the south,

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:50.480
fled north and arrived to this
village to find empty homes

00:20:50.480 --> 00:20:52.291
and this Christian church.

00:20:52.291 --> 00:20:54.791
(piano music)

00:20:56.350 --> 00:20:57.990
And they converted it into a mosque

00:20:57.990 --> 00:21:01.250
so that they could continue
their religious practices.

00:21:01.250 --> 00:21:06.160
But eventually they built a
new mosque, right next door.

00:21:06.160 --> 00:21:09.203
And they left this
church totally abandoned,

00:21:10.210 --> 00:21:13.960
where it lays today, falling apart.

00:21:13.960 --> 00:21:16.460
(piano music)

00:21:17.970 --> 00:21:20.720
Both sides suffered immense losses.

00:21:20.720 --> 00:21:24.980
And now empty buildings are
the most visceral solid symbol

00:21:24.980 --> 00:21:26.203
of that loss.

00:21:28.710 --> 00:21:31.010
These abandoned churches
are peppered all throughout

00:21:31.010 --> 00:21:32.461
the occupied north,

00:21:32.461 --> 00:21:34.961
(piano music)

00:21:42.269 --> 00:21:45.352
(campfire crackling)

00:21:47.470 --> 00:21:50.820
But perhaps the most haunting
symbol of this switching

00:21:50.820 --> 00:21:54.940
of sides happened here, in that
thriving Greek tourist town

00:21:54.940 --> 00:21:56.120
called Famagusta.

00:21:56.120 --> 00:21:58.170
- [Newscaster] Famagusta
was the tourist resorts

00:21:58.170 --> 00:21:59.970
of the island. The major port,

00:21:59.970 --> 00:22:02.450
much of the wealth of Greek
Cyprus was based here.

00:22:02.450 --> 00:22:04.880
Last weekend, the Greeks fled in panic

00:22:04.880 --> 00:22:06.380
before the Turkish tanks.

00:22:06.380 --> 00:22:09.160
They have abandoned all that they have

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:13.137
and they won't return.
Now, the town is deserted.

00:22:13.137 --> 00:22:15.887
(ethereal music)

00:22:21.350 --> 00:22:22.585
- We're about to go in.

00:22:22.585 --> 00:22:24.610
I've been thinking about this place for,

00:22:24.610 --> 00:22:26.013
the better part of a decade.

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:28.370
And we're about to go in.

00:22:32.090 --> 00:22:35.220
The reason I spent months
trying to get access to film

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:37.960
in this ghost town.
Wasn't just to capture it,

00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:40.900
but rather to understand the human tragedy

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:44.370
that happened here. So I asked
this man to come with me.

00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:46.248
His name is Mr. Takis,

00:22:46.248 --> 00:22:50.160
he's a Greek Cypriot who
was my age back in 1974.

00:22:50.160 --> 00:22:53.150
He lived here in
Famagusta. He owned hotels.

00:22:53.150 --> 00:22:56.440
He was in the tourism industry,
but when Turkey invaded,

00:22:56.440 --> 00:22:58.380
he did what everyone
else did in this town.

00:22:58.380 --> 00:22:59.970
He left with his family.

00:22:59.970 --> 00:23:03.642
And today he's returning
for the first time ever.

00:23:03.642 --> 00:23:07.323
- This is not easy to bear.

00:23:09.623 --> 00:23:12.550
It brings out anger. It
brings out confusion.

00:23:12.550 --> 00:23:17.210
It brings out disappointment.
My wife refused to come.

00:23:17.210 --> 00:23:19.920
I came with my daughters
today. I came with them, but

00:23:21.249 --> 00:23:24.140
they tell me "you don't talk."

00:23:24.140 --> 00:23:26.413
I lost my, I cannot connect.

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:33.490
- Can you paint a picture to
me on what Famagusta was like?

00:23:33.490 --> 00:23:35.773
- The economy was so strong here.

00:23:36.810 --> 00:23:40.190
Not only economically, Johnny,

00:23:40.190 --> 00:23:43.310
but culturally, educationally.

00:23:43.310 --> 00:23:45.923
It was a wonderful period.

00:23:47.180 --> 00:23:48.310
- I asked Mr. Takis,

00:23:48.310 --> 00:23:51.130
if you would be comfortable
showing me his old home.

00:23:51.130 --> 00:23:53.080
So we started walking through what used

00:23:53.080 --> 00:23:54.850
to be his entire world,

00:23:54.850 --> 00:23:58.920
the cafe he used to come to
often, his brother's old house,

00:23:58.920 --> 00:24:00.400
where he went to school.

00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:02.530
- I spent some of my best years yet.

00:24:02.530 --> 00:24:03.363
- Wow.

00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:08.230
The school is now boarded
up and covered with Turkish

00:24:08.230 --> 00:24:10.820
and TRNC flags.

00:24:10.820 --> 00:24:15.780
- To see this in this situation
makes me very, very sad.

00:24:15.780 --> 00:24:17.690
- As we walked through this ghost town,

00:24:17.690 --> 00:24:19.350
I kept trying to get Mr. Takis

00:24:19.350 --> 00:24:22.547
to go back to the Famagusta
of before the invasion

00:24:22.547 --> 00:24:25.950
and to point out the places
that he had good memories from.

00:24:25.950 --> 00:24:28.830
What do you remember about
that time. But after a while,

00:24:28.830 --> 00:24:30.833
he finally just stopped me.

00:24:31.720 --> 00:24:35.890
- Johnny, I told you before,
I cannot connect. I cannot.

00:24:35.890 --> 00:24:37.720
You are asking about my memories.

00:24:37.720 --> 00:24:39.200
- Yeah.

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:40.453
- They don't bring out,

00:24:41.320 --> 00:24:43.300
the full memories I want to describe

00:24:43.300 --> 00:24:45.800
because, there is a reservation,

00:24:45.800 --> 00:24:47.300
this is not the town I lived.

00:24:47.300 --> 00:24:49.880
This is not the town that I was proud of.

00:24:49.880 --> 00:24:50.713
- Yeah.

00:24:50.713 --> 00:24:53.700
- This is not the town
that has tremendous history

00:24:53.700 --> 00:24:54.823
and success.

00:24:57.005 --> 00:24:58.093
It is unbelievable.

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:04.020
- On that day that everyone had to flee,

00:25:04.020 --> 00:25:06.120
you thought you'd be coming back.

00:25:06.120 --> 00:25:07.490
- Yes, for sure.

00:25:07.490 --> 00:25:09.630
Everybody who left, thought
they were going away

00:25:09.630 --> 00:25:10.550
for two, three days.

00:25:10.550 --> 00:25:13.200
They didn't take anything
more than clothes and food.

00:25:16.370 --> 00:25:19.480
- We walked through this
city deep in conversation.

00:25:19.480 --> 00:25:22.460
And then Mr. Takis's
friend, who was with us,

00:25:22.460 --> 00:25:25.130
signaled that we were
getting close to the house.

00:25:25.130 --> 00:25:28.730
The one he hadn't seen since
he fled all those years ago,

00:25:28.730 --> 00:25:32.170
Mr. Takis didn't pick up that
we were getting so close.

00:25:32.170 --> 00:25:34.783
And when he looked up,
he realized we were here.

00:25:35.980 --> 00:25:37.380
Tell me about this building?

00:25:42.020 --> 00:25:43.020
- What should I say?

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:46.400
- I could tell he felt caught off guard.

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:49.363
And I began to regret having
set this whole thing up.

00:25:50.620 --> 00:25:52.940
- Well, you didn't prepare me.

00:25:52.940 --> 00:25:54.380
- [Johnny] I was subjecting this man

00:25:54.380 --> 00:25:58.020
to a traumatic reopening
of his old wounds.

00:25:58.020 --> 00:26:00.970
All so I could make my
video about the conflict.

00:26:00.970 --> 00:26:02.670
It started to not feel right,

00:26:02.670 --> 00:26:04.820
but he assured me that we could keep going

00:26:04.820 --> 00:26:06.090
and that he wanted to do this.

00:26:06.090 --> 00:26:08.763
- No, I want to go on the other side.

00:26:09.823 --> 00:26:10.850
Let's go from the beach.

00:26:10.850 --> 00:26:12.570
- He didn't want me to see his home

00:26:12.570 --> 00:26:15.360
from this angle, from
this bombed out street.

00:26:15.360 --> 00:26:17.610
Instead he wanted to see
it from the other side,

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:18.630
from the beach,

00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:21.220
the part of this town that
still has some semblance

00:26:21.220 --> 00:26:22.400
of its former glory.

00:26:22.400 --> 00:26:24.313
- Not from here. From the other side.

00:26:25.570 --> 00:26:28.588
I didn't realize, we
had reached the house.

00:26:28.588 --> 00:26:31.190
- Yeah, It looks like there's
an entrance over here, maybe.

00:26:31.190 --> 00:26:35.760
- Now you will get, an idea
of why it was so popular.

00:26:35.760 --> 00:26:36.593
I think seeing.

00:26:36.593 --> 00:26:38.173
- [Johnny] Yeah, I haven't
seen the beach yet.

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:44.320
- This is the famous
sandy beach of Famagusta.

00:26:44.320 --> 00:26:47.560
- As we walked towards his
house. Now from the beach side,

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:50.880
it was clear that Mr.
Takis still felt deep pride

00:26:50.880 --> 00:26:54.390
for this place, this beach, this city.

00:26:54.390 --> 00:26:58.020
- In certain areas, there
is even turquoise water.

00:26:58.020 --> 00:26:59.663
- It's stunning. It's beautiful.

00:27:00.670 --> 00:27:04.560
And though his city and his
property and his stories are now

00:27:04.560 --> 00:27:07.690
a ghost town, occupied
by an invading power.

00:27:07.690 --> 00:27:09.630
When you're on this beach,
you don't feel it as much.

00:27:09.630 --> 00:27:12.660
- Beaches like this, there
are not many in the world.

00:27:12.660 --> 00:27:13.680
- No there really aren't.

00:27:13.680 --> 00:27:15.363
- Especially with turquoise water.

00:27:16.671 --> 00:27:19.421
(ethereal music)

00:27:22.800 --> 00:27:25.580
- And then after a moment
walking along this beach,

00:27:25.580 --> 00:27:28.753
we finally approached his home.

00:27:34.910 --> 00:27:36.760
This is the first time you're seeing this.

00:27:36.760 --> 00:27:39.960
- The first time I have
seen it after 47 years.

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:44.063
It hurts me, I cannot
connect in the sense that,

00:27:45.410 --> 00:27:50.410
what I see doesn't bring
out all those nice memories

00:27:50.730 --> 00:27:52.330
and the good times that you had.

00:27:53.460 --> 00:27:56.523
You have to close your eyes
and then it all comes back.

00:27:59.300 --> 00:28:01.210
- [Newscaster] Famagusta
was the tourist resort

00:28:01.210 --> 00:28:02.043
of the island.

00:28:03.510 --> 00:28:05.607
All this belonged to the Greeks.

00:28:12.181 --> 00:28:14.493
Famagusta this morning, was a ghost town.

00:28:14.493 --> 00:28:17.141
It is unlikely that the
Turks will allow them back.

00:28:17.141 --> 00:28:18.808
- We didn't prepare.

00:28:20.390 --> 00:28:24.770
My wife still refuses to come

00:28:25.910 --> 00:28:27.800
because it's painful.

00:28:27.800 --> 00:28:29.310
I come here to do what?

00:28:29.310 --> 00:28:32.913
Like now we see it and
I cannot go into it.

00:28:35.880 --> 00:28:36.923
What is the purpose?

00:28:38.620 --> 00:28:42.003
I'm not a tourist. It's
my home. It's my town.

00:28:43.200 --> 00:28:46.173
And I come here and you can't
go here, you can't go there.

00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:49.573
You can see your house from distance.

00:28:51.140 --> 00:28:53.893
It's a torture, It's a torture.

00:28:55.610 --> 00:28:57.730
I know it's difficult for everybody

00:28:57.730 --> 00:28:59.400
to get into somebody else's shoes,

00:28:59.400 --> 00:29:01.657
but I'm sure you appreciate
that it hurts a lot.

00:29:01.657 --> 00:29:03.853
It hurts a lot, hurts a lot.

00:29:05.370 --> 00:29:06.203
It's not right.

00:29:08.810 --> 00:29:12.010
- I watched Mr. Takis take
a photo with his daughters,

00:29:12.010 --> 00:29:15.573
who he had brought to see the
house they almost grew up in,

00:29:16.810 --> 00:29:19.340
they stood in front of the
Turkish military border

00:29:19.340 --> 00:29:21.993
that bars them from actually going inside.

00:29:22.830 --> 00:29:27.123
And in that moment, I felt a
profound sadness for it all.

00:29:28.840 --> 00:29:30.970
Unlike anything that I'd felt in the years

00:29:30.970 --> 00:29:32.829
of studying this conflict.

00:29:32.829 --> 00:29:35.980
(campfire crackling)

00:29:35.980 --> 00:29:38.780
Those conversations are so
important because like this isn't

00:29:38.780 --> 00:29:41.680
just a international relations issue.

00:29:41.680 --> 00:29:43.420
This is like a human issue.

00:29:43.420 --> 00:29:44.810
It's like people and their identity

00:29:44.810 --> 00:29:47.440
and their homes being lost because

00:29:47.440 --> 00:29:49.660
of great power conflicts. To me,

00:29:49.660 --> 00:29:51.390
it's much more important
to have those types

00:29:51.390 --> 00:29:53.840
of conversations then
just to be zoomed out,

00:29:53.840 --> 00:29:54.743
looking at a map.

00:29:57.420 --> 00:30:01.020
Both Greek and Turkish Cypriots
suffered immense violence

00:30:01.020 --> 00:30:03.610
and loss of their homes and communities

00:30:03.610 --> 00:30:06.600
in 1974 and the years leading up to it.

00:30:06.600 --> 00:30:09.270
And throughout this series,
I want to shed light,

00:30:09.270 --> 00:30:10.940
not just on this history,

00:30:10.940 --> 00:30:14.180
but what it feels like to
live in this place today.

00:30:14.180 --> 00:30:17.560
A country divided into
four, full of buffer zones

00:30:17.560 --> 00:30:20.060
and barriers and division.

00:30:20.060 --> 00:30:20.893
At the end of the day,

00:30:20.893 --> 00:30:24.380
this is a division, that was
constructed by outside powers,

00:30:24.380 --> 00:30:26.320
which is the story of Cyprus.

00:30:26.320 --> 00:30:29.750
The story of a strategically
placed island that everyone

00:30:29.750 --> 00:30:31.610
wanted and was ripped apart,

00:30:31.610 --> 00:30:33.934
purely because of where it was located.

00:30:33.934 --> 00:30:36.434
(piano music)

00:30:52.730 --> 00:30:56.950
Okay, well, you made it through
episode one of Uncharted,

00:30:56.950 --> 00:31:00.120
this pilot series of something that I want

00:31:00.120 --> 00:31:03.040
to do a lot more of. There
are three more episodes.

00:31:03.040 --> 00:31:04.540
So this is just the beginning.

00:31:05.600 --> 00:31:08.980
This is a new form of
journalism that I am pushing.

00:31:08.980 --> 00:31:13.390
And I'm really grateful to
the sponsor who supported it,

00:31:13.390 --> 00:31:15.340
which is Cometeer.

00:31:15.340 --> 00:31:17.470
Cometeer is kind of blowing my mind.

00:31:17.470 --> 00:31:20.620
They're a completely
different version of coffee

00:31:20.620 --> 00:31:22.560
than anything I've ever experienced.

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:24.443
The way it works is that Cometeer,

00:31:24.443 --> 00:31:28.290
works with some of the
best artisanal roasteries

00:31:28.290 --> 00:31:29.770
around the world.

00:31:29.770 --> 00:31:32.050
And they make beautiful, wonderful coffee.

00:31:32.050 --> 00:31:33.270
They brew it themselves,

00:31:33.270 --> 00:31:35.540
but then they immediately flash freeze it.

00:31:35.540 --> 00:31:38.590
Like they freeze it in these
insanely low temperatures

00:31:38.590 --> 00:31:40.470
to like seal in the freshness

00:31:40.470 --> 00:31:42.770
and the aromas and the flavors

00:31:42.770 --> 00:31:43.920
of this roasted coffee.

00:31:43.920 --> 00:31:47.110
And then they put it in
these little metal capsules

00:31:47.110 --> 00:31:49.863
and send them to your house, frozen.

00:31:50.850 --> 00:31:53.470
They show up, you pull
them out of these capsules.

00:31:53.470 --> 00:31:56.200
You put them into a cup with hot water

00:31:56.200 --> 00:31:58.470
and you have delicious coffee,

00:31:58.470 --> 00:32:00.940
that is like the coffee that was brewed.

00:32:00.940 --> 00:32:02.160
I mean, it's, it's brilliant.

00:32:02.160 --> 00:32:04.053
And it tastes really good.

00:32:05.660 --> 00:32:07.080
Listen, I'm a big coffee guy.

00:32:07.080 --> 00:32:08.930
And I like to think
that I have like the art

00:32:08.930 --> 00:32:11.600
of the pourover down, with like my scale

00:32:11.600 --> 00:32:13.430
and like my timer and all this stuff.

00:32:13.430 --> 00:32:14.740
Honestly, it takes a long time,

00:32:14.740 --> 00:32:17.380
which is fine on a weekend.
But like during the week,

00:32:17.380 --> 00:32:20.740
like I sort of want a
more convenient option.

00:32:20.740 --> 00:32:24.200
I could pay for a coffee shop to make it

00:32:24.200 --> 00:32:25.580
and have it delivered to my house,

00:32:25.580 --> 00:32:30.090
which costs me a lot of
money or I could pay way less

00:32:30.090 --> 00:32:34.300
and have a really good cup
of coffee that is reliable

00:32:34.300 --> 00:32:36.230
and sustainably packaged.

00:32:36.230 --> 00:32:38.330
And all of this stuff through Cometeer.

00:32:38.330 --> 00:32:39.920
Cometeer is kind of a new thing.

00:32:39.920 --> 00:32:42.530
They're not fully open to the public yet.

00:32:42.530 --> 00:32:44.300
There's some waitlist.

00:32:44.300 --> 00:32:45.750
If you want to bypass that waitlist,

00:32:45.750 --> 00:32:46.940
there's a link in my description,

00:32:46.940 --> 00:32:49.770
it is cometeer.com/johnnyharris,
that allows you

00:32:49.770 --> 00:32:53.820
to bypass the waitlist and get
50% off your first purchase,

00:32:53.820 --> 00:32:57.350
which makes this like $1 per cup,

00:32:57.350 --> 00:32:59.060
which is insanely cheap for the quality

00:32:59.060 --> 00:33:00.410
of coffee you're getting.

00:33:00.410 --> 00:33:01.780
So if this is interesting to you,

00:33:01.780 --> 00:33:03.070
go to the link in my description,

00:33:03.070 --> 00:33:05.040
clicking that link helps
support this channel,

00:33:05.040 --> 00:33:07.290
but it also gives you that 50% off

00:33:07.290 --> 00:33:09.370
and allows you to bypass the waitlist.

00:33:09.370 --> 00:33:11.550
I feel really excited
that I discovered this

00:33:11.550 --> 00:33:15.130
and we'll be using it for
the foreseeable future.

00:33:15.130 --> 00:33:18.150
So thank you Cometeer
for sponsoring this video

00:33:18.150 --> 00:33:19.920
and thank you all for being here.

00:33:19.920 --> 00:33:22.870
There are three more
episodes of Uncharted coming

00:33:22.870 --> 00:33:25.110
to you in the next few weeks.

00:33:25.110 --> 00:33:27.801
So keep an eye out for
those. See you later.

00:33:27.801 --> 00:33:30.468
(trumpet music)

